Title: The Island
Order: 1
Production Code: 1x01

EXT. LAKE ERIE, NEAR BUFFALO - LATE AFTERNOON, A JUNE DAY

PAGE: 1 (SPLASH)

PANEL: Full-page splash, from high above the water looking north over the Niagara frontier: the white mist of the Falls at the top of the page, Buffalo's harbor with its steamers and canal boats at the lower left, the dark line of the Canadian shore across the top right, and in the lower center a single birch canoe on a blue, glittering Lake Erie. In it, a brown-skinned boy paddles and a girl with two long plaits leans over the side trailing her hand. Heat shimmer; it is uncommonly hot for the lakes.
LIGHT: Late sun, low and golden, dazzling off the water

CAPTION: Crossing the Niagara river in a direct line, the Canadian shore lies not more than eight miles from Buffalo, New York.

CAPTION: In the early 50's, small bands of Indians were still familiar figures on both borders.

PAGE: 2

PANEL: Buffalo's sandy beach: Seneca women in gaudy blankets sit in the sun in spite of the heat, beaded wares spread on the white sand, steamers and canal boats behind them.

CAPTION: Buffalo was an anti-slavery stronghold.

PANEL: A busy street. Men in buckskin and moccasins with feathers; a blue-eyed man with a glint of gold in the hair under a tribal hat; a free Black man in a good coat; clerks, sailors, a lady with an umbrella. Cosmopolitan crowd, nobody looking twice.

CAPTION: The last and most convenient station of the underground railroad.

PANEL: Close on the canoe from the water level. JUDAH, about sixteen, paddles with total ease: lithe brown body, moccasins, a young brave's clothes without feathers or paint, curly, crispy hair in the sun. WINONA, a child (the novel gives her as under fourteen), trails a slim brown hand in the water; embroidered dark blue dress, two long plaits of sunny hair.

PANEL: Ahead of them a small wooded island near the bigger shadow of Grand Island: balsam and hemlock down to a pebbly beach, a wooden cottage chimney barely showing over the trees.

CAPTION: The island was the home of White Eagle.

PAGE: 3

PANEL: The canoe grates on pebbles. Judah hauls it up the beach; Winona carries her moccasins in her hand and a stringer of trout hangs from a rod on his shoulder with the gun. Balsam thicket in the background where the canoe is always hidden.

PANEL: Inset flashback, sepia-green: a younger WHITE EAGLE, a white man in buckskin and Seneca dress, kneels beside a feverish chief RED EAGLE in a bark lodge, a cholera sickness all around them. A medicine bag at his belt.

CAPTION: A white man who had saved the life of the Seneca chief Red Eagle in a time of cholera, and was adopted by the tribe.

PANEL: Inset flashback: White Eagle on the same island in 1842, hauling logs for a four-room cottage, with an old half-deaf NOKOMIS beside him carrying a bucket.

CAPTION: When the Indians gave up Buffalo Creek in 1842, he took this small island, and an old woman as his housekeeper.

PANEL: Inset flashback: White Eagle at the door of the cottage as a handsome, well-educated woman in a plain dress comes up the path carrying a small child in her arms, a long road behind her.

CAPTION: Then he brought home a mulattress who had escaped from slavery by the underground railroad.

CAPTION: With her was a child whose mother had died on the road to freedom.

PAGE: 4

PANEL: Inset flashback: A small Canadian church. White Eagle and the woman at the altar rail; the minister raising his hands; a little boy of five with curly hair watching from the front pew.

CAPTION: He crossed to the Canadian shore and married her, by English law, with the sanction of the Church.

CAPTION: The child he adopted and called Judah.

PANEL: Inset flashback: the cottage bedroom at dusk. The woman lies in bed, pale, with a baby in the crook of her arm; White Eagle at her side with his head in his hands.

CAPTION: In a short time after the birth of Winona, the wife sickened and died.

PANEL: Inset flashback: White Eagle on the beach at sunrise lifting a toddler Winona high overhead while a boy Judah grips his coat and old Nokomis laughs.

CAPTION: Winona was queen of the little island, and her faithful subjects were her father, Judah and old Nokomis.

PANEL: Back to the present: Judah and Winona climbing a wooded slope in dappled sun, squirrels and blackbirds, pushing through ferns. Judah is singing; the girl follows him closely.

PAGE: 5

PANEL: On the crest of the ridge. They stand looking down at the bay and the Canadian houses small on the far shore, the Falls a faint white plume on the horizon.

SFX: BOOOM

PANEL: Winona drops on her knees in the grass; a flash of pale pink among the green.

PANEL: Close on the Indian-pipes: faint pink stems, gauzy white cups, the flowers pushing up through dead leaves. The two children's hands part the leaves together.

WINONA
Look at them. Oh, Judah, are they not beautiful?

PANEL: Judah looks at the flowers and then, for a moment longer than he means to, at her face glowing with enthusiasm; her wide brow, the hair in rich dark rings, a hint of pink in her olive skin like the flowers'. A beat of something he doesn't have a name for.

JUDAH
But they turn black as soon as you touch them.

PANEL: Winona shakes her head, stubborn, protective. Judah's hand hovers over the stem.

WINONA
Yes, I know; but we will leave them here where they may go away like spirits. Old Nokomis told me.

JUDAH
Old Nokomis! She's only a silly old Indian squaw. You mustn't mind her stories.

PAGE: 6

PANEL: Judah drops on his stomach in tall grass with the gun, aiming at a pigeon in a tree. Winona behind him with her hands clasped.

PANEL: Close on Judah's eye along the barrel; the pigeon in the sights, perfect.

WINONA (O.P.)
Did they tell you that at school?

PANEL: The pigeon explodes upward; Judah rolls over, furious. Winona stands with her chin out.

JUDAH
There now! You've spoilt it! Why did you speak?

WINONA
Well, I wanted to know.

PANEL: Walking again, down the far slope, Judah carrying the fish, pompous.

JUDAH
In school you learn not to believe all the silly stories that we are told by the Indians.

PANEL: In the cove: a rude stone fireplace, a pile of dry leaves, a hidden store of tin cups and a kettle under a rock shelf. Winona has stopped.

WINONA
I tell you, Judah, I will not go to the convent school. I hate nuns.

JUDAH
Ho, ho! But you must; the father has said it.

PAGE: 7

PANEL: Winona stamps a small foot on the sand like a little whirlwind, eyes wet.

WINONA
Papa cannot make me. I will not.

PANEL: Judah, solemn and amused, holds up three fingers.

JUDAH
What, again! That makes three times since morning that you've been off like a little fury.

PANEL: Winona wipes her face with her wrist, half laughing, half ashamed.

WINONA
I know it, Judah, but you are so tantalizing. You'd make a saint lose her temper, you know you would.

PANEL: Judah stops, pointing out over the water. Three dark birds hang in the deep blue toward the island.

JUDAH
Look, Winona! Gulls!

WINONA
No! No! They're eagles, Judah!

PANEL: Wide on the lake. Two eagles swing away toward the mainland; the third drops into a maple on the shore. Judah is already catching up his gun.

PAGE: 8

PANEL: Judah leans forward to take aim at the young eagle; Winona at his shoulder.

JUDAH
It's a young eagle, Winona, and I'm going to drop him!

PANEL: A puff of white smoke from behind a bend in the shore below them, a little way off.

SFX: KRAK

PANEL: The eagle leaps one stroke in the air and drops like a stone into the water.

PANEL: A boat shoots out from the beach with two men in it; they row hard to where the bird floats and haul it in.

PANEL: The children watching from the high rocks, small against the sky. Winona has taken hold of Judah's sleeve.

PAGE: 9

LAYOUT: Three tiers; the bottom two panels are the same wide frame as a conversation in a doorway.

PANEL: The boat grounds on the beach. Two white men in hunters' dress step out: THOMSON, black-haired, thin-lipped, curved nose; and a LEADER, tall, powerful, dark hair and a beard just starting to whiten, deep-set eyes that don't look straight. The Leader (this is COLONEL TITUS, unnamed here) carries the dead eagle by its feet.

PANEL: Winona shrinks behind Judah, glancing at them from under the clustering curls. Judah stands very straight.

LEADER
Oh, then it is not a part of the Canadian shore?

JUDAH
This island has no name. Not that I ever heard. It's just an island.

PANEL: Thomson eyes the boy's head with an expression Judah can't read. The Leader speaks pleasantly; his eyes do not.

LEADER
Do you live here?

JUDAH
Yes, over there.

PANEL: The other man, hat on the back of his head, sprawls on a rock.

THOMSON
We're mighty hungry. We'll pay you well for your fish.

JUDAH
You are welcome to as much as you wish.

PAGE: 10

PANEL: At the cove the children cook: Winona hangs trout on leather strings over a good bed of coals; Judah brings wood. The two men sprawl on a bed of dry leaves with their pipes.

WINONA
Show them our fireplace, Judah.

PANEL: Close on the two men, in the foreground and out of earshot, heads together, smoke wreathing, the children small and busy behind them at the fire.

THOMSON
Likely nigger.

LEADER
Worth five hundred, sure. But the girl puzzles me. What is she?

PANEL: The Leader watches Winona through rings of smoke; Thomson slaps his thigh.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
She's a nigger, too, or I'll eat my hat!

LEADER
By George! Thomson, you don't suppose we've struck it at last!

PANEL: Thomson's wink, enormous, then Winona turns with a plate of birch bark and a fish.

THOMSON
Mum's the word.

PANEL: Judah, fingering the eagle's feathers with a sullen look he can't explain to himself.

LEADER (O.P.)
You haven't told us who you are.

PAGE: 11

PANEL: Judah's face, closed. Winona beside him with her plate.

JUDAH
She's White Eagle's daughter; I'm adopted.

LEADER
I see. Then you're Indians?

PANEL: Judah nods, uneasy. The men chew, watching.

PANEL: Close on Thomson speaking low and bitter to the Leader as the boy turns away.

THOMSON
Not by a long sight. Nothin' but nigger blood ever planted the wool on top of that boy's head.

PANEL: In the woods behind the cove, a horn blown from somewhere high and far; ripples of sound drawn as faint curving lines through the tree trunks.

SFX: HOOOOOOO

PANEL: The children bolt into the trees without a word to the strangers, and are gone in the dark shadows.

PAGE: 12 (TURN)

PANEL: The two men on their leaves, looking after the children.

THOMSON
Well, cap't, this is a rum un. Now what do you reckon that means?

PANEL: The Leader, quiet, with the dead eagle across his knees; behind him the sun going down red over the lake.

LEADER
I have an idea that we've struck it rich, Thomson.

PANEL: Wide on the beach as the men push their boat out into the water, alone.

LEADER (CONT'D)
Come, unless we want to stay here all night, suppose we push out for civilization?

EXT. BUFFALO TURNPIKE - NIGHT (JULY)

PANEL: A month later, black sky and a road beaten to mud. Lightning forks across the top of the panel and shows a lone rider on a tired horse, flat felt hat, duck and corduroy, spattered to the knees. The turnpike runs toward a few lights.

CAPTION: One sultry evening in July, about a month later.

PANEL: Close on WARREN MAXWELL: slender, well-knit, golden hair plastered to his forehead, blue eyes, a dimple in the chin. He laughs at himself in the lightning.

WARREN
Heavens, what a country!

PAGE: 13

LAYOUT: This page is a flashback in warm lamplight, framed with a thin border so it reads as memory.

PANEL: Inset: a law office in London. MR. PENDLETON, white-haired and kind, behind a desk full of papers, speaks to Warren, standing hat in hand.

CAPTION (WARREN): With the idea of suiting his dress to the country, I had fitted myself out in Regent Street.

CAPTION (WARREN): The old aristocrat, my father, said anything was better than trade.

PANEL: A rich man's dining room: Sir John Maxwell, a baronet, with three older sons at a long table. Warren, the youngest, alone at the end.

PENDLETON (V.O.)
Some one in our confidence must go to America. It is on a delicate mission relating to the heir of Carlingford.

PANEL: Pendleton with a sheaf of papers, his hand on a miniature of two young men in uniform. Behind him a window in the rain.

PENDLETON
It was a bad business when young Lord George and his brother fell in love with the same woman.

PENDLETON (CONT'D)
Captain Henry and Miss Venton had settled the matter to their own liking; but the lady's father favored the heir.

PANEL: Pendleton's story, drawn as a flash: a grand room, a quarrel, two brothers. In the foreground a woman in white, hands over her mouth, turning away.

PENDLETON (V.O.)
Soon after a terrible quarrel, Lord George was found dead, shot in the back through the heart.

PAGE: 14

PANEL: A courtroom: a handsome officer in the dock, calm. A judge, a jury, the foreman standing.

PENDLETON (V.O.)
The Captain was arrested, tried and convicted. I remember the trial well. He was a bonny and gallant gentleman.

PANEL: A prison window, a rope of knotted sheets, the officer's boots over the sill. A ship on the water far below.

PENDLETON (V.O.)
Yes, he escaped from prison and fled to America.

PANEL: Pendleton again, tapping the papers with a finger; Warren listens, serious.

PENDLETON
Old Lord George cannot live many months longer; he is eighty-five.

PENDLETON (CONT'D)
But he thinks that Captain Henry may have married in America, and if so, he wants his children to inherit.

PANEL: Close on Warren's face, hat still in hand, then a flash of his father's old face and the family estate, then back to the rainy road. The lamplight border breaks.

PENDLETON (V.O.)
Your mission must remain a profound secret. If he lives, Captain Henry is yet amenable to the law which condemned him.

PANEL: Back in the storm. Warren straightens in the saddle. Ahead of him a large wooden house with outbuildings and a long piazza and one lit window.

WARREN
Ah, what is that yonder?

EXT. GRAND ISLAND HOTEL - NIGHT

PAGE: 15

PANEL: The hotel's door swings open in rain and lamplight. A gray-bearded, large, good-natured man in shirtsleeves stands in the doorway. From the side of the house, powerful 'TAVIUS comes at a run.

MAYBEE
Well, stranger, you've took a wet night fer a hossback ride.

PANEL: Warren dismounts, pleasant. Maybee takes his saddle-bags himself.

WARREN
I find it so. May I have shelter here until morning?

MAYBEE
Shelter! That's what the Grand Island Hotel hangs out a shingle fer.

PANEL: In the bar-room: bottles, a stove, a counter. Maybee reads a visiting card by a tallow candle, tilting it.

MAYBEE
"Warren Maxwell, England." Um, um, I s'pose you're an 'ristocrat. Where bound? Canidy?

WARREN
No, just travelling for pleasure.

PANEL: A table in the hotel parlor, a smoking dish of fried chicken, a johnny-cake and coffee; Warren at it, exhausted and already half asleep over it.

MAYBEE (O.P.)
Yer supper'll be 'bilin on the table inside a half-hour.

PANEL: Warren's room: a plain bed, a candle burning down, his boots at the foot; he is asleep, his arm over his face. Rain at the glass.

SFX: KNOCK KNOCK KNOCK

PAGE: 16

PANEL: The corridor in darkness. Warren in trousers and shirt, one hand on the wall, a faint voice of weeping coming up from below.

PANEL: The bar-room by one candle. Two figures in the light: JUDAH in Indian dress, hands stiff at his sides, and WINONA, about fourteen but looking younger, weeping. Maybee has two lanterns in his hands.

MAYBEE
It's White Eagle's son and daughter. Something has happened him and they want me to go with them to the island.

PANEL: Close on Warren seeing Winona's face for the first time: wide soft brown eyes full of tears, piteous.

WARREN
What seems to be the trouble with your father?

JUDAH
I believe he's shot himself, sir.

PANEL: Winona clinging to the landlord's hand, tears streaming.

WINONA
O, come, Mr. Maybee. My father--oh, my father!

PANEL: Warren, pulling on his coat, resolved.

WARREN
If you will wait a moment I will go with you. I know something of medicine, and delay may be dangerous.

PANEL: Winona turns to him with a pleased cry; Maybee buckles his boots, gruff.

MAYBEE
You don't know what you're about. You must remember, mister, that these people are only niggers and Injuns.

PAGE: 17

PANEL: Warren stops in the corridor, shocked.

WARREN
Niggers! Mr. Maybee, what do you mean?

PANEL: Maybee, matter-of-fact, lantern light on his face.

MAYBEE
The boy is a fugitive slave picked up by White Eagle and adopted. The girl is a quadroon.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
Her mother, the chief's wife, was a fugitive too.

PANEL: Warren, with the lantern swaying across his face, thinking of everything he has read about American slavery.

WARREN
Still they're human beings, and entitled to some consideration.

CAPTION: For all his hard words, Ebenezer Maybee was a humane man, and had done much for the very class he assumed to despise.

PANEL: Full-width: the party at the water's edge in the rain, four small figures and two lanterns; the white hissing lake and the black sky, lightning silhouetting the island across the water.

PAGE: 18 (SPLASH)

PANEL: Full-page splash: the open boat in the storm, tilted up the face of a wave, foam flying. Judah stands upright in the bow, shoving off with a pole, hair streaming; Maybee and Warren at the oars; Winona crouched small in the bottom of the boat with a blanket over her head. Lightning forks behind and flashes across the white water.
LIGHT: Lightning white, everything else near black

CAPTION: Not even an Indian would ordinarily trust himself to the mercy of the water on such a night.

MAYBEE (O.P.)
Judah knows his business. He'll take us over all right if anybody can.

SFX: KRAKOOOM

PAGE: 19

EXT. WHITE EAGLE'S ISLAND - NIGHT

PANEL: The cottage door thrown open by Winona; the bare room, a rough board floor, rude fireplace spouting flames, a grease lamp. On a bed of skins, a still shape. Nokomis rocking to and fro, moaning.

NOKOMIS
Ah! my bird! It is no use--it is too late.

PANEL: Winona stands in the door, rigid.

WINONA
What do you mean, Nokomis?

NOKOMIS
White Eagle has answered the call of the Great Spirit.

PANEL: Winona gives one heart-breaking cry and falls; Warren catches her before she hits the floor.

WINONA
Dead! My father!

PANEL: Warren, with Winona in his arms, follows Judah to the other room and lays her on a bed; Judah stands at the door, shaken.

JUDAH
How will she bear it when she gets back her senses? How shall we both bear it?

PANEL: In the kitchen: the body on the skins, Warren stripping back the hunting shirt, Maybee holding the lantern. A fine, handsome man of about sixty, well preserved.

PAGE: 20 (TURN)

PANEL: Close on Warren's hand at the dead man's back, the lamp light on blood. His face has gone white.

PANEL: Warren rising, looking straight at Maybee.

WARREN
This is no accident.

PANEL: Wide, the room frozen: Nokomis aghast, Maybee with his mouth open, the dead man between them, rain loud on the roof. Warren's last line is lettered large at the top, over the drawing.

WARREN (CONT'D)
It is murder!

SFX: KRAKOOOM
